{
  "dom_id": "step-q344800",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14215565/assets/5b73cc737e5a07d6517431a41ad81d478e54c963b8295d4fb0996b6a33a3b34e.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+1}$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above). Find the equation defining the $x$-coordinate of the point $P.$\n\n$2x\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+1}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+(\\sqrt{x+1})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+1 & =0 \\\\ 2x+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
